## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and cross-platform memory barrier requirements in a production system context.

## Key Concepts
- MPMC ring buffer with per-slot sequence numbers
- ABA problem mitigation via publish/subscribe pattern
- Architecture-specific memory barrier requirements
- Contention handling and wraparound correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for enqueue/dequeue operations
bool enqueue(struct ring *r, void *data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Wait for slot to be available
    while (r->slots[slot].seq != pos) {
        // Backoff or handle contention
    }

    // Write payload
    r->slots[slot].data = data;

    // Publish with release barrier
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, pos + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}

bool dequeue(struct ring *r, void **data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Check if slot is ready with acquire barrier
    size_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if (seq != pos + 1) return false;

    // Read payload
    *data = r->slots[slot].data;

    // Advance head
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->head, pos + 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return true;
}
```

## Memory Barriers by Architecture
- **x86_64**: `memory_order_release`/`memory_order_acquire` (no additional barriers needed)
- **ARM64**: `dmb ish` for release, `dmb ishld` for acquire operations

## Validation Approach
- Stress testing with multiple producers/consumers under high contention
- ThreadSanitizer and AddressSanitizer for race condition detection
- Formal verification using TLA+ or similar model checking tools
- fuzzing with random production/consumption patterns

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of race conditions, safe resource reuse, and lightweight OS primitives in a beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts

- Bitmaps for ID allocation
- Generation counters to distinguish reuse
- Simple locking strategy to avoid races
- Handling exhaustion and graceful error paths

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  uint16_t id;      // 0..65535
  uint16_t gen;     // generation counter
  bool in_use;
} pid_slot;

// Global state (simplified)
pid_slot table[65536];
pthread_mutex_t pid_lock;

int alloc_pid(void) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&pid_lock);
  for (uint32_t i = 1; i < 65536; i++) {
    if (!table[i].in_use) {
      table[i].in_use = true;
      table[i].gen++;
      int pid = (table[i].gen << 16) | i;
      pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
      return pid;
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
  return -1; // exhausted
}

void release_pid(uint32_t pid) {
  uint16_t idx = pid & 0xFFFF;
  pthread_mutex_lock(&pid_lock);
  table[idx].in_use = false;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
}
```

